<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="erythritol biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Accept-If-Predicted Pathways (AIPP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are present widely throughout the plant kingdom. The AIPP list contains pathways that are automatically accepted if they are predicted for any plant species database. However, AIPP pathways are not automatically imported into databases if they are not predicted by the Pathologic program. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: ERYTHRITOL Erythritol| is produced by microorganisms as an osmoprotectant, and occurs naturally in small quantities in mushrooms, fruits such as pears, grapes, and watermelons, and in fermented foods. Erythritol is a four-carbon polyol that is used a natural sweetener in the food and pharmaceutical industries. It exhibits about 70% of the relative sweetness of sucrose, and was approved as low calorie sweetener by the FDA in 2001. The majority of erythritol (ca. 90%) is not metabolized in the human body and is excreted into urine within 48 h after oral intake, resulting in an extremely low energy value (0.2 - 0.4 kcal/g) |CITS: [28954540]|. Erythritol is produced on an industrial scale through the fermentation of sugars by some yeasts, such as |FRAME: TAX-64607| sp., |CITS: [28954540]|. As of 2018, the world production of erythritol was about 60000 ton/year |CITS: [32095676]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; Yeast produce |FRAME: ERYTHRITOL| as an osmoprotectant. |FRAME: ERYTHRITOL Erythritol| is biosynthesized from |FRAME: ERYTHROSE-4P|, derived from the |FRAME: PENTOSE-P-PWY|. The pathway consists of the dephosphorylation of |FRAME: ERYTHROSE-4P| to |FRAME: ERYTHROSE| followeed by reduction to |FRAME: ERYTHRITOL| at the expense of NAD(P)H |CITS: [20529366]|. The pathway is used commercially for the production of |FRAME: ERYTHRITOL|. The isolation of |FRAME: TAX-203381| in 1988 (then called &lt;i&gt;Aureobasidium&lt;/i&gt; sp. SN-124A) |CITS: [Wako88]|, followed by the development of a mutant with enhanced production |CITS: [Ishizuka89]| lead to the industrial production of erythritol in Japan in 1990. In contrast to ethanol, erythritol hardly suppresses the growth of yeasts, and a high concentration of erythritol (&gt;200 g/L) can be obtained in a batch fermenter |CITS: [19169680]|. Erythrose reductase has been identified and described in a few erythritol-producing yeasts including: Torula sp. |CITS: [12200310]|, |FRAME: TAX-203381| |CITS: [Tokuoka92][23294575]|, |FRAME: TAX-5490| |CITS: [12839736][20529366]|, and |FRAME: TAX-4952| |CITS: [28693571]|. Using stable isotope tracing it has been shown that human erythrocytes are capable of erythritol synthesis from glucose via the pentose phosphate pathway |CITS: [28484010]|. Both the mammalian alcohol dehydrogenase 1 (ADH1) and sorbitol dehydrogenase (SORD) reduce |FRAME: ERYTHROSE| to |FRAME: ERYTHRITOL| using NADPH |CITS: [31511322]|.</Description>
