      <Comment source="AU4FS-4, SRI, caspi">Erythritol is produced by microorganisms as an osmoprotectant, and occurs naturally in small quantities in mushrooms, fruits such as pears, grapes, and watermelons, and in fermented foods. Erythritol is a four-carbon polyol that is used a natural sweetener in the food and pharmaceutical industries. It exhibits about 70% of the relative sweetness of sucrose, and was approved as low calorie sweetener by the FDA in 2001. The majority of erythritol (ca. 90%) is not metabolized in the human body and is excreted into urine within 48 h after oral intake, resulting in an extremely low energy value (0.2 - 0.4 kcal/g) |CITS: [28954540]|. Erythritol is produced on an industrial scale through the fermentation of sugars by some yeasts, such as |FRAME: TAX-64607| sp., |CITS: [28954540]|. As of 2018, the world production of erythritol was about 60000 ton/year |CITS: [32095676]|. Despite having two stereo centers, erythritol is a completely symmetric compound, and even though one could find literature that refers to D and L forms, they are in fact identical.</Comment>
